import{_ as t,o,c as i,a2 as s}from"./chunks/framework.CGSzHL4z.js";const p=JSON.parse('{"title":"Limitations","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"reference/limits.md","filePath":"reference/limits.md","lastUpdated":1792705540000}'),n={name:"reference/limits.md"};function a(r,e,d,l,h,c){return o(),i("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[s('<h1 id="limitations" tabindex="-1">Limitations <a class="header-anchor" href="#limitations" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Limitations&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Some of AcesMotion&#39;s behaviour comes from Roblox itself. The table below pairs each engine limit with what AcesMotion does about it; the current limits of AcesMotion follow.</p><h2 id="engine-limitations-and-how-acesmotion-works-around-them" tabindex="-1">Engine limitations, and how AcesMotion works around them <a class="header-anchor" href="#engine-limitations-and-how-acesmotion-works-around-them" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Engine limitations, and how AcesMotion works around them&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Roblox does this</th><th>So AcesMotion does this</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>A joint&#39;s <code>Transform</code> never replicates between server and clients.</td><td>Every client evaluates joints itself. The server sends one message per play with the server clock instead of a stream of poses.</td></tr><tr><td>A Roblox animation holds one rig&#39;s joints and nothing else.</td><td>Cameras, parts, properties, sounds and effects play through the runtime from scene data. Joint-only scenes still export natively.</td></tr><tr><td>Studio&#39;s Animator samples a CurveAnimation 30 times a second.</td><td>Export defaults to KeyframeSequence, which plays every key exactly.</td></tr><tr><td>In edit mode a joint&#39;s Transform moves nothing until the Animator steps.</td><td>The preview steps the Animator after every pass so poses show while you scrub.</td></tr><tr><td><code>IKControl</code> doesn&#39;t solve in edit mode.</td><td>AcesMotion has its own IK solver, used for pins, context IK and every preview.</td></tr><tr><td>Studio&#39;s Move and Rotate tools rewrite a dragged part&#39;s Motor6D.</td><td>AcesMotion poses with its own gizmo and holds the viewport mouse while a scene is open.</td></tr><tr><td>Studio keeps Delete, Ctrl+C / V / X / D and a few others even while a plugin window has focus, and a plugin can&#39;t read global shortcut bindings.</td><td>Defaults avoid those keys (copy is <kbd>Shift+C</kbd>, paste <kbd>Shift+V</kbd>), and the Timeline sets Studio&#39;s selection aside while the pointer is over it, so a stray Delete can&#39;t remove your rig.</td></tr><tr><td>A plugin can&#39;t see keys held in its own window through <code>UserInputService:IsKeyDown</code>.</td><td>Modifiers come from each input event instead.</td></tr><tr><td>Studio&#39;s PluginMenu can&#39;t grey out or tick items.</td><td>AcesMotion draws its own menus, so every item can explain why it&#39;s disabled.</td></tr><tr><td>Studio&#39;s Undo discards every unrecorded change since the last step.</td><td>The preview rests the stand-ins around every undo step and poses them again after.</td></tr><tr><td>A Sound&#39;s <code>Play()</code> does nothing in edit mode.</td><td>The preview plays hidden AudioPlayer copies with the Sound&#39;s effects.</td></tr><tr><td>Studio can&#39;t fast-forward a particle emitter.</td><td>The preview shows frozen particle copies for the frame you&#39;re on.</td></tr><tr><td>Roblox&#39;s Animation Graph can&#39;t be authored by plugins.</td><td>Out of scope. Scenes and layers handle blending instead.</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="current-limits" tabindex="-1">Current limits <a class="header-anchor" href="#current-limits" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Current limits&quot;">​</a></h2><h3 id="keys-and-focus" tabindex="-1">Keys and focus <a class="header-anchor" href="#keys-and-focus" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Keys and focus&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li>Studio keeps <strong>Delete, Ctrl+C / V / X / D, Shift+X, O, <code>,</code> <code>.</code></strong> for itself even while a plugin window has focus, and any key bound in File &gt; Customize Shortcuts is global. A plugin cannot read those bindings, so any default can clash on someone&#39;s machine: rebind in Settings (four keys) or in Studio&#39;s shortcut editor. If a Timeline key does nothing, the status bar says &quot;Click the Timeline first, then press the key again ({name}).&quot;</li><li>The Timeline takes keyboard focus when the pointer enters it; after leaving it, Studio&#39;s <code>W</code> / <code>E</code> / <code>R</code> need a click in the viewport first.</li><li>On some setups a key may not reach the plugin: the Timeline taking focus on hover (its hover keys still work over it whichever window has focus), <code>Tab</code> (temporary pivot) in the viewport (Studio&#39;s own draggers use it), <code>J</code> over the viewport, and <code>Ctrl+Shift+O</code>. Studio installs can also bind keys like <code>I</code>, <code>K</code> and <code>V</code> to other actions, which is why the hold keys can be rebound.</li><li>Studio&#39;s own tools (Move, Rotate) must not be used on a posed stand-in: they rewrite the rig&#39;s joint offsets. Posing holds the mouse so that cannot happen by accident.</li><li>Settings has no Navigation page yet (scroll and zoom presets); there is no UI for the zoom and pan bindings.</li><li>Studio&#39;s own Ctrl+C, V, X, D, Z and Y can&#39;t be given to an AcesMotion command (Studio takes them first). Studio has no API for a plugin to open its File &gt; Customize Shortcuts editor.</li><li>Settings &gt; Shortcuts lists keys only, not mouse gestures (scroll, drags): those are in section 5 below.</li><li>Crash recovery keeps one copy per scene (up to 20), written 2 s after each edit; the interval isn&#39;t a setting, how long copies are kept is (Settings &gt; Storage).</li></ul><h3 id="editor-limits" tabindex="-1">Editor limits <a class="header-anchor" href="#editor-limits" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Editor limits&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li><strong>Editor windows</strong>: 6 per kind; the 7th is refused with &quot;Close an editor window first&quot;. Sequence editor: 20 points at most; gradient editor: 20 stops at most; Saved strip: 64 colours, 32 gradients, 32 sequences; saved names up to 24 characters.</li><li>Sequence blending (two NumberSequences or ColorSequences) reads both at every keypoint time and keeps at most 20 points.</li><li>Call count 1 to 50. Sound speed 0.25x to 4x (Ctrl stretch; a key can carry 0.05 to 20). A sound row stacks up to 4 lanes; row-height extra is 0 to 240 px.</li><li>Multi-edit: at most 500 objects.</li><li>Zoom 0.01 to 64 px per frame (zoomed far out, a 30 minute scene at 60 fps fits in 1000 px; the ruler labels every 10,000 frames or 10 minutes if it has to). Snap distance 1 to 32 px. Onion Before / After 0 to 10 in Settings (1 to 5 in the menu), Step 1 to 120. Gizmo size 50 to 200%. Bone thickness and joint dot size 25 to 300%, their transparency 0 to 90%. Trajectory line 1 to 8 px, point size 50 to 300%.</li><li>A Roblox animation holds one rig and joints only: cameras, parts, property tracks, calls, attributes, root motion kept, modifiers and audio need the runtime (layers bake into the clip with <strong>Bake layers</strong> on, else the clip is Base alone); the export panel lists them. Markers export natively (the payload as JSON text, 64 characters).</li><li><strong>Studio samples a CurveAnimation on a fixed 30 per second grid</strong>, so a Constant step or a 10 ms spike softens; KeyframeSequence (the default) plays every exported keyframe exactly.</li><li>BrickColor, Font and PhysicalProperties values cannot be keyed. Content properties are keyed through their ContentId twin. CFrame properties are posed in the viewport (the Inspector shows a read-only position); CFrame attributes are editable.</li><li>Mirror: a joint whose rest frame does not mirror its partner&#39;s exactly is left out of Paste mirrored; the status line counts them. IK limits cover elbows and knees only. Resize works on parts and models, not rigs. Limbs only ghosts cannot split a skinned mesh.</li><li>A motion trail samples every frame up to 600 samples across all paths (longer spans sample every few frames).</li></ul><h3 id="previews" tabindex="-1">Previews <a class="header-anchor" href="#previews" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Previews&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li><strong>Effect preview</strong> shows particles as frozen copies on the frame you are on (Studio cannot fast-forward an emitter): the arrangement may re-roll when you pause; real emitters run at 1x and slower while playing; above 1x or in reverse the frozen copies take over. Flipbooks step through their frames by a render-by-render timing trick, so a frame can be one off now and then. Particles only age on rendered frames, so check timing with the viewport showing. A scrolling Beam holds still while scrubbing (Roblox restarts a Beam&#39;s scroll whenever its speed is set). The preview cap (100 to 10,000, default 1000) keeps every k-th particle; real playback and games always show all.</li><li><strong>Sounds</strong>: a Sound&#39;s own Play() does nothing in edit mode, so AcesMotion plays hidden AudioPlayer copies (3D by default, flat with &quot;Play sounds in 2D while animating&quot;) with the Sound&#39;s effects and SoundGroups. While scrubbing only short snippets play, not continuous audio.</li><li><strong>Waveforms</strong> are read in 2 second chunks as they scroll into view; a sound&#39;s length arrives in about half a second, until then its bar is a short placeholder.</li><li>The Loading spinner on sound bars does not spin.</li><li>Marker payloads cannot be edited in the UI yet (they come from imports).</li></ul>',11)])])}const m=t(n,[["render",a]]);export{p as __pageData,m as default};
